# Composer Flow Mode & Bus Discovery [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

> The left pane toggles between **Layout** (pixel placement) and **Flow** (bus wiring). Flow makes the invisible visible: which widgets publish/subscribe which namespaces, and — critically — what indeterminate traffic is *live on the ComposeBus right now* that a widget could listen for. Without this, widgets are deaf to the outside world. Pre-vis: `specs/ux/composer-flow-previs.v2.html`.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-FL-INTENT] Make the Bus Wiring Visible and Listenable

- definition: A node-graph view of the design's bus topology, on a Layout↔Flow toggle in the Widget Workshop. It is a *synced projection* of the same `.compose.yaml` as Layout/Bindings/Source (DP-CXC-2/7) — edit in any, all stay in lockstep; not a new authority.
- key-insight: ComposeIO is already a graph — namespaces are channels, widgets publish/subscribe. The node view is its honest representation, and it is the natural home for *bus discovery*: choosing what a widget listens for out of open, indeterminate traffic.
- status: operational (2026-07-07) — C1–C9 all PASS (31 compliance tests in `tests/render/test_flow_mode.py`; suites 307 macOS / 303+4skip RPi5); live MQTT smoke 13/13 from BOTH platforms (`tests/render/smoke_flow_discovery_mqtt.py` — a genuinely separate ComposeApp peer on mqtt-broker.local); 11-step walkthrough screenshots macOS↔RPi5 (`tests/render/drive_flow.py` → captures/flow/, 12–24 px/shot Δ≤3, all in the known stroked-circle AA class; the exported artifact byte-identical).
- implemented: `composer/flowpanel.py` (FlowPane = Bus·Live rail + node graph, ONE interactive widget; FlowInspectorPanel = the WC **Flow** tab; `build_graph(core)` = the pure raw-spec→graph projection) + `composer/discovery.py` (BusDiscovery, the live registry) + the Layout|Flow topbar toggle. Lens + selected connection are BUS STATE (`composer/ww/mode` enum, `composer/flow/selected` json — both MQTT-imported); the WC is now 6 tabs (…/Flow).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

---

## 2. The Graph Model

### [CXC-FL-MODEL] Namespaces Are First-Class Nodes

- definition: Nodes are (a) **widgets** with attribute ports, (b) **namespaces** rendered as fan-in/fan-out hub nodes, and (c) **behaviors** (optional, as nodes on a widget). Wires are bindings/subscriptions. Data flows **publisher widget → namespace node → subscriber widget's attribute**.
- rationale: a namespace hub honestly models the bus — one namespace can have many publishers and many subscribers, which a direct widget-to-widget wire cannot express, and it hides nothing (the bus decoupling is the point). "Wire an attribute to a variable" = drag from the attribute port to a namespace node; the wire IS the binding.
- sync: the graph reads/writes the CompositeDocument's RAW spec (per [CXC-BE-RAWSPEC]) — never re-serialize from the parsed tree.
- test: a design's bindings render as widget↔namespace wires; dragging a new wire writes the binding into the `.compose.yaml`; the Layout/Bindings/Source views reflect it.
- implemented: `build_graph(core)` derives nodes/wires ONLY from `doc.bindings()` + declared namespaces (discovery decorates liveness) — so Intelligence-authored and UI-authored wiring are the identical graph by construction (C9 test asserts node/wire-set equality AND byte-equal YAML). FlowNode.kind ∈ namespace|widget|logic — the [CXC-FL-HEADLESS] hook is structural, a widget node carries only id/type/ports. Behaviors render as `» behavior` ports on the widget node (option (c) folded in, not separate nodes — one node per widget keeps the graph legible at this scale). Drag-to-wire: rail-row → widget node (the node EXPANDS to show all bindable ports mid-drag, so the drop picks the exact attribute — ports come from `bindable_attrs_for()`, zero hardcoding) → emits the simplest [SPEC:CXC-BIND] form via `composer/config/widget_changed`; rail-row → open canvas = declare/subscribe only.
- change: "Flow's rail + graph are ONE engine widget (FlowPane), not two panels."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "[SPEC:CXC-BINDEDIT] deferred drag-connect because a cross-panel drag needs an engine drag/ghost-wire primitive (escalation boundary)."
  - reasoning: "Making the rail a REGION of the Flow widget keeps every drag pointer-down→pan→release inside one widget's existing gesture surface — drag-to-wire ships without new engine mechanics, and the previs's layout (rail flush against the canvas) is what the design wanted anyway."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That rail→WC drags (e.g. rail row → Bindings tab) never become necessary; those would still need the cross-panel primitive."
- fix-found: `composer/core._h_widget_changed` coerced a BARE-STRING `visible` binding (the sanctioned simplest form) to the Layers-eye bool — the binding was silently dropped on the command path (raw-spec loads were unaffected, which is why BINDEDIT's round-trip tests missed it). Now only bools + the bool vocabulary drive the eye; other strings stay RAW ([CXC-BE-RAWSPEC]).
- status: operational (2026-07-07)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

---

## 3. Bus Discovery

### [CXC-FL-DISCOVERY] Listen for Indeterminate External Traffic

- definition: a **Bus·Live rail** shows namespaces observed *live on the ComposeBus* (fed by bus monitoring), INCLUDING traffic this design never declared — e.g. an ESP32 emitting `jumpdrive/readiness`. Each entry shows its namespace, state engine, last value, and a live pulse.
- three kinds, visually distinct: **declared-internal** (this design's own), **subscribed-external** (an outside namespace this design listens to), **live-available** (on the bus, not yet wired).
- two authoring acts:
  - **discover-and-wire** — the signal is already live; drag it onto the canvas and wire it to an attribute.
  - **declare-expected** — subscribe to a namespace *before* it's live (the rig isn't powered yet); the widget waits and lights up the instant the signal arrives.
- model note: the binding model must mark a namespace binding as declared-internal vs subscribed-external, and the Bindings-tab namespace picker should ALSO offer "browse the live bus" (this is a binding-model gap, not only a Flow feature).
- test: an external namespace published by another bus participant appears in the rail within one poll; dragging it wires a subscription; a declare-expected binding to a not-yet-live namespace activates when it first publishes.
- implemented: `composer/discovery.py` BusDiscovery over the design/external bus — `subscribe_pattern("*", …)` (state + later namespaces) + `bus.on_message` (event liveness, publisher source) + `_bus/discovery` harvest. Registry rows: engine/value/first_seen/last_seen/count/source/app_id; kinds computed against the document (declared-internal / subscribed-external / **expected** (declared, never observed) / live-available); tool plumbing (`_bus/*`, `_app/*`, `composer/*`) excluded. Binding-model addition landed: **`NamespaceDef.external: bool`** — serialized as an additive `external: true` (omitted when False, so pre-FLOW documents round-trip byte-identically); wire-authoring sets it when the namespace is live-but-undeclared or a pattern. The Bindings-tab picker gained a **LIVE ON THE BUS** chip group (same registry), and its `_ensure_declared` now declares external + the observed engine. Both authoring acts real: drag (discover-and-wire, incl. drop-on-open-canvas = subscribe only) and the rail's **"+ listen for (typed name)"** affordance when the filter text names something not live (declare-expected).
- fixes-found (bus tier, landed with this pass):
  - `bus.on_message` monitors NEVER saw transport arrivals (incoming MQTT dispatched straight to namespace objects) — a "bus monitor" deaf to the outside world; `_wire_transport_incoming` now notifies monitor callbacks, so every BusMonitorWidget sees external traffic too.
  - an UNKNOWN external namespace was auto-created with the `binary` default engine, so any non-binary outside value (`tri_level` "charging") raised and was silently dropped — auto-created-from-external namespaces now get the permissive `json` engine; a REGISTERED engine rejecting an external value logs a peer-contract warning instead of killing dispatch.
- engine-legibility ([CXC-FL-ENGINE]): `ComposeApp._publish_discovery` now includes an ADDITIVE `engines: {ns: engine_type}` field, so a peer's exports are legible (smoke-verified: the rig's `tri_level` shows in the rail, source "peer"); otherwise the engine is inferred from observed values and displayed with an honest `~` prefix.
- status: operational (2026-07-07) — MQTT smoke: an undeclared namespace from a separate ComposeApp peer appears live with engine+value; declare-expected flips to subscribed-external on first publish (C7 test drives the previewed widget with it).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

### [CXC-FL-FILTER] Filter the Firehose (P0 — load-bearing, not polish)

- definition: over time the bus carries a LOT of namespaces; without filtering, discovery is unusable and widgets are effectively deaf. Filtering has several axes, all standard:
  - **keyword / substring** on the name;
  - **namespace-prefix glob** (`jumpdrive/*`, `ship/dradis/*`) — leverages ComposeIO's hierarchical namespaces for free grouping/collapse;
  - **category** — external-vs-internal, by engine type, "active in the last N seconds";
  - a **showing N of M** context line.
- priority: P0. This is the difference between discovery working with 5 namespaces and with 500.
- test: with 60+ live namespaces, a `jumpdrive/*` filter narrows the rail to the matching set; a keyword search matches substrings; the count reflects the filter.
- implemented: `BusDiscovery.filter(text=, category=)` — substring, trailing-glob (bus `pattern_matches` semantics), fnmatch for other `*` forms; categories all/external/internal/active(≤10s)/live/wired + engine-type names. Rail UI: search LiveInput + chip row (**All** + up to 3 prefix chips COMPUTED from live traffic (`prefix_chips()` — hierarchical namespaces give grouping for free) + external/active/in-design) + the **"showing N of M"** line. C5-tested at 62 namespaces: `jumpdrive/*` → 3 of 62.
- status: operational (2026-07-07)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

### [CXC-FL-PATTERN] A Pattern Is Both a Filter and a Subscription

- key-insight: the glob you type to *discover* (`jumpdrive/*`) is also an expression a widget can *bind to* — listen to a whole **family** of signals, not one namespace. One language serves discovery (UI) and subscription (runtime): a BusMonitor of all `alert/*`, a tile reacting to any `jumpdrive/*` change, a dradis consuming every `dradis/contact/*`.
- test: a widget bound to a pattern receives updates from every matching namespace, including ones that appear after the binding is authored.
- change: "NEW BUS PRIMITIVE (ESCALATED, **RATIFIED by David 2026-07-07**): `ComposeIOBus.subscribe_pattern(pattern, cb)` — trailing-glob-only wildcard state subscription, with a transport hook (`Transport.subscribe_pattern`, default no-op; MQTT maps to its native `#` wildcard). Full transport-abstraction (in-process / MQTT / CAN) + the CAN hardware-acceptance-mask model is specified in **[SPEC:CXC-PATSUB]** (`21-composebus-pattern-subscription.lts`)."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The bus had ONLY exact-name subscription; the MQTT transport subscribed `composeio/+/{ns}` per known namespace (plus `_bus/#`), so traffic for a namespace this process never registered NEVER ARRIVED — discovery (C4) and family subscription (C8) were impossible without a bus addition. The kickoff marked this ESCALATE-don't-guess."
  - reasoning: "Built the MINIMAL primitive whose semantics the bus already implies rather than inventing new ones: `prefix/*` = the prefix and its whole subtree, `*` = everything — exactly MQTT's `#` semantics, so `jumpdrive/*` maps 1:1 to topic filter `composeio/+/jumpdrive/#` and matches the hierarchical-namespace design intent. Delivery = the plain subscribe callback signature with the ACTUAL member namespace passed; fires on every matching publish (liveness, old may equal new — discovery needs the pulse, and re-applying a binding is idempotent). Mid-pattern wildcards (`a/*/b`) raise ValueError — deliberately NOT bus semantics; extending them is a spec change, not a convenience."
  - rejected_alternative: "Discovery via `_bus/discovery` announcements only (no wildcard) — rejected: it hears only well-behaved ComposeApp peers, not arbitrary/legacy traffic, and cannot carry last values or satisfy C8 at all."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That trailing-glob covers the real use-cases (family = subtree). If per-level selection (`ship/+/temp`) is ever needed, that is a NEW escalation. Also assumes wildcard MQTT reception at Composer scale is affordable; on very busy brokers a scoped default filter may be needed."
  - implication: "Semantics note: `p/*` also matches `p` itself (MQTT-`#`-aligned). A declared pattern 'namespace' is NOT registered as a literal namespace anywhere (runtime + Composer both skip it); it exists only as a subscription/declaration. `unsubscribe_pattern` removes the callback; the transport-level wildcard persists (harmless surplus, deduped)."
- implemented: bus (`bus.py`: `is_pattern`/`pattern_matches`/`validate_pattern`, `_pattern_subs` notified on local publishes AND transport arrivals) · MQTT (`transport_mqtt.py`: `_pattern_topic` + reconnect re-subscribe; `ComposeApp._add_transport` re-widens late transports) · runtime (`engine/runtime.py`: widget `namespace:`, attr bindings, and behavior triggers all pattern-wire) · WW preview (`composer/chrome.py`: identical wiring on the design bus) · authoring (a `namespaces:` entry may BE a pattern, `external: true`, e.g. the exported `namespace: jumpdrive/*` bus_monitor).
- status: operational (2026-07-07) — C8 verified at bus, engine (incl. a family member created AFTER wiring), preview, and real-MQTT tiers; family attr-bindings are last-writer-wins across members (documented).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

### [CXC-FL-ENGINE] State-Engine Awareness Makes Indeterminate Signals Legible

- role: to interpret an unknown external signal you must know its shape. A discovered namespace surfaces its **state engine** (binary / tri_level / continuous_0_255 / enum / json), and the value-map editor ([CXC-BIND-C8]) translates its values into what the widget uses (`cold/charging/spooled` → gauge value + color). Without the engine an arbitrary message is noise.
- implemented: engine resolution priority **declared > peer > inferred** — the design's own NamespaceDef; the peer's `_bus/discovery` announcement (via the NEW additive `engines:` field in `ComposeApp._publish_discovery`); else inferred from observed values with an honest `~` prefix (`~binary`/`~continuous_0_255`/`~json`/`~string`). Shown in the rail row, hub node subtitle, Inspector, and `bus_live` snapshot. Wire-authoring declares the discovered engine, so the value-map editor gets the right vocabulary. Known limit: a raw (non-ComposeApp) publisher with string states reads `~string` until a human/Intelligence sets the engine on the declaration.
- status: operational (2026-07-07)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

---

## 4. Scale & the Connection Inspector

### [CXC-FL-SCALE] Keep Big Graphs Legible

- items: auto-layout, grouping by module, a minimap, filter-driven focus (show only one namespace's connections). The right-pane **Inspector** shows the selected connection — source namespace, engine, first-seen, and its `when`/`map`/`default` — i.e. exactly "what am I listening for."
- implemented (first pass): two-column auto-layout (namespace hubs left, widgets right; node drag re-positions ephemerally), graph scroll, live wires animate flow-dashes, selected wire highlights its endpoints. **Inspector = the WC "Flow" tab** (FlowInspectorPanel; the WC follows the lens — entering Flow auto-switches to it): flowline chips → listening-for card (live dot, engine, first-seen age, publisher, outside-vs-own) → Source field → when/invert/map/default rows from the RAW spec → **Edit in Bindings** / **Unbind** actions → "also on the bus, not yet wired" (top live-available).
- deferred: minimap, module grouping, filter-driven graph focus (dim non-matching), persistent node positions — revisit when a real design outgrows two columns.
- status: operational (first pass, 2026-07-07)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

### [CXC-FL-HEADLESS] Forward Hook — Headless Widgets Are Nodes Here

- note: declarative headless *logic* widgets (their own forthcoming spec) appear in this graph as logic nodes alongside visual widgets and namespace hubs. Flow mode is their authoring environment — together they form a visual, declarative, bus-native logic surface. Design the node model so a non-visual widget node fits without special-casing.
- implemented (the hook only): `FlowNode.kind` ∈ `namespace | widget | logic`; widget nodes are built from id/type/ports with zero geometry semantics, so a headless widget is `kind="logic"` + its ports — no special-casing (unit-tested). For the Headless slug: `build_graph` will need the headless widget's in/out port declarations from its (future) introspection table, and hubs should then support widget→namespace **output** wires as first-class (today publish wires exist in the model but only Button-class widgets author them).
- status: hook landed (2026-07-07); the spec itself is future work
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FLOW]

